# Milestone: v0.10.12-cat-e-activation
# Covers: D-slotchange-1 (program-order emit) + D-slotchange-2 (FIFO subscriber
#         walk) determinism rules (spec #4 §7): multiple subscribers on the same
#         slot-change event receive notification in registration order; M writes
#         maintain that order across all firings.  Run under all 3 tunable
#         presets × 100 runs to assert byte-identical output.
#
# blocked: no multi-preset runner mode.  The four prerequisites this
#          header used to list -- top-level `var`, Object.new(),
#          `at (obj.x.changed?)`, `obj.x = N` -- all work now; the
#          single-run form of the claim is covered by
#          reactive/slot-change/.  What is missing is the repetition and
#          preset sweep the determinism claim needs.
#
# Everything below this line predates the re-foundation: it names
# tasks and entry points that no longer exist, and its account of
# what stands in the way has been superseded by the `blocked:` line
# above, which was probed against this branch.  Kept for the
# original urbiscript it carries.
# matrix-row: reactive-slot-change
#
# Reason:
#   The fixture requires:
#     - var declaration at top level (globals exposure, T69)
#     - Object.new() constructor (globals + stdlib, T70+)
#     - at (obj.x.changed?) event-subscribe watcher (globals for `obj`)
#     - obj.x = N assignment (globals for `obj`)
#   All of the above require the T69 globals-exposure task.  The underlying
#   urbi_emit_slot_change_if_subscribed fast-path + slow-path chain walk were
#   tested in unit/test_slot_change_emit.c and
#   unit/test_slot_change_callsites.c until the Phase 0 runtime-internals test
#   cleanup; no C-level coverage today.
#
# Activate at T69 by replacing the source below with the uncommented version.
#
# Urbiscript source (activate at T69):
#   var o = Object.new();
#   var seq = "";
#   at (o.x.changed?) function (v) { seq = seq + "A" + v };
#   at (o.x.changed?) function (v) { seq = seq + "B" + v };
#   o.x = 1 | o.x = 2 | o.x = 3;
#   echo(seq);
#   --- expected output ---
#   [00000001] "A1B1A2B2A3B3"
